    \u0425\u0430\u0439\u044f\u043c, \u0441\u0443\u0434\u044f \u043f\u043e \u0435\u0433\u043e
    \u043f\u043e\u044d\u0437\u0438\u0438, \u0432\u0435\u043b \u043a\u043e\u0447\u0435\u0432\u043e\u0439
    \u043e\u0431\u0440\u0430\u0437 \u0436\u0438\u0437\u043d\u0438, \u0431\u044b\u043b \u043f\u043e\u0433\u0440\u0443\u0436\u0435\u043d
    \u0432 \u043d\u0430\u0443\u043a\u0443 \u0438 \u0444\u0438\u043b\u043e\u0441\u043e\u0444\u0438\u044e
    \u0438, \u0432\u0435\u0440\u043e\u044f\u0442\u043d\u043e, \u043d\u0435 \u0441\u0442\u0440\u0435\u043c\u0438\u043b\u0441\u044f
    \u043a \u0441\u043e\u0437\u0434\u0430\u043d\u0438\u044e \u0431\u043e\u043b\u044c\u0448\u043e\u0439
    \u0441\u0435\u043c\u044c\u0438. \u041e\u043c\u0430\u0440 \u0425\u0430\u0439\u044f\u043c
    \u043f\u0440\u0435\u0434\u0441\u0442\u0430\u0435\u0442 \u043f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0434 \u043d\u0430\u043c\u0438
    \u043a\u0430\u043a \u0447\u0435\u043b\u043e\u0432\u0435\u043a, \u0434\u043b\u044f \u043a\u043e\u0442\u043e\u0440\u043e\u0433\u043e
    \u0433\u043b\u0430\u0432\u043d\u044b\u043c \u0432 \u0436\u0438\u0437\u043d\u0438 \u0431\u044b\u043b\u043e
    \u0441\u043b\u0443\u0436\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435 \u043d\u0430\u0443\u043a\u0435 \u0438 \u0438\u0441\u0442\u0438\u043d\u0435,
    \u0430 \u0435\u0433\u043e \u043b\u0438\u0447\u043d\u044b\u0435 \u043f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0436\u0438\u0432\u0430\u043d\u0438\u044f
    \u0438 \u0431\u044b\u0442\u043e\u0432\u044b\u0435 \u043f\u043e\u0434\u0440\u043e\u0431\u043d\u043e\u0441\u0442\u0438
    \u043e\u0441\u0442\u0430\u043b\u0438\u0441\u044c \u0437\u0430 \u0440\u0430\u043c\u043a\u0430\u043c\u0438
    \u0438\u0441\u0442\u043e\u0440\u0438\u0438."
  personalLifeEn: "The life of Omar Hayyama, captured in the remaining sources, is seen as the path of a traveling scientist who devoted himself entirely to science and left a bright mark in mathematics and astronomy, and his personal life remains largely behind the pages of a chronicle focused on his intellectual achievements, and the main milestones of his biography, collected from the pieces of historical evidence that have come to our attention, are presented below.\n\nThe origin and early years of Omar Haiyam, whose full name was Giyazaddin Abu-L-Fath Omar Bin Ibrahim Nishapuri, was born on 18 May 1048 in Nishapur, then a major cultural center of Khorasan (north-east of modern Iran), and his family, according to the name (Hayama, tent master), was made from craftsmen who made tents, and he was known to have a younger sister named Aisha.At the age of 12, Omar entered the Nishapura madrasa, where he mastered Islamic law and medicine with the skill of a doctor (hakima), but he was not attracted to medical practice, and he devoted himself entirely to mathematics and to studying the work of ancient and eastern scientists such as Euclid and Avizenna (Ibn Xina).At the age of 16, Omar's life changed dramatically, with his father and mother dying during the epidemic, selling his parents' home and workshop, he left Nishapur and went to Samarkand, one of the main scientific centres in the East.\n\n♪ The journey to glory: The study and work in Samarkand Hayam continued in madrasas, but his training in public debates was so impressed by the teachers that he was immediately promoted to the rank of mentor, as was many scientists of the time, he did not stay long in one place, and about 1070 he moved to Buhara, where he worked for about 10 years in local bookstores.This fruitful period allowed him to write four fundamental mathematical tracts.\n\nIn 1074, when he was about 27 years old, he was invited to Isfahan, the capital of the powerful Seljuk Empire, and was invited by the Sultan of Malik Shah and his first Viser, Nizam al-Mulka, one of the legends, to be the classmates of Hayyama in his youth, and in Isfahan, Hayam was the leader of one of the world's largest palace observatorys and became the spiritual mentor of the Sultan.This period (about 20 years) was the top of his career, and working with a group of scientists, he: - he developed a solar calendar called \"Jalali\", which was more accurate than the modern European calendar, and is still in use in Iran and Afghanistan. - He compiled \"Malixachian Astronomical Tables\", which included a catalogue of bright stars.- He wrote his famous work \"Commentaries to the Hard Postulates of the Euclid Book\" (1077), where he studied the theory of parallel lines and the teaching of numbers, following the ideas of non-euclidic geometry.\n\nThe students, colleagues and relationships of Hayam with other scholars are few known. Among his disciples were well-known scholars, such as Al-Asfizari and Al-Hazini.His philosophical beliefs, especially his scepticism about religion, have caused displeasure to the orthodox clergy, and he has been repeatedly accused of godless free thought.\n\nZakat: The banishment, recent years, and death of the Brilliant Isfahan period ended in 1092 with the death of his patrons, the Sultan Malik Shah, and the Visir Nizam al-Mulka. The period of turmoil and power struggle began.He has spent the last years of his life there, living in a private way, teaching and research, and he has been shown to be living in poverty, without support, as a hermit in fact. Omar Hayam died on December 4, 1131, at the age of 83.When he reached the section of \"Universal,\" he stopped and laid a page with a toothpick, and closed the book, and made a will, and turned to prayer, and said, \"I have tried to know You as I can, and forgive me; I have come to know You, and I have come to You.\"\n\nPersonal life: The question of Hayam's personal life remains one of the main mysteries of his biography. Sources remain almost silent on this subject.Children: The story of the daughter, which appears in some later books, is a literary fiction.Hayam himself, according to his poetry, has been a nomadic, has been put into science and philosophy and probably has not sought to create a large family, and Omar Haiyam has come before us as a man who has been at the centre of his life to serve science and truth, and his personal experiences and domestic details have been left out of history."
  shortBioRu: "\u041e\u043c\u0430\u0301\u0440 \u0425\u0430\u0439\u044f\u0301\u043c \u2014 \u0444\u0438\u0433\u0443\u0440\u0430,
